{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "241",
    "case_summary": "A woman in her 50s presented with 4 days of blurry vision and pain in her left eye after undergoing a routine dental cleaning 1 week prior. She had no history of gingival disease or cavities. Her medical and ocular histories were unremarkable.\n\nVisual acuity was 20/25 OD and 20/50 OS. Intraocular pressures were 15 mm Hg OD and 11 mm Hg OS. Left eye examination showed conjunctival hyperemia, faint keratic precipitates, 3+ cells with fibrin in the anterior chamber, mild nuclear sclerosis, and a whitish focal granuloma at the 4-o'clock position at the pupillary margin. The posterior segment was unremarkable, with no evidence of vitritis, retinitis, or vasculitis. The right eye was unremarkable. At 1-day follow-up, visual acuity declined to 20/100 OS, with development of hypopyon and increased fibrinous material emanating from the iris lesion. No posterior segment involvement was noted.\n\n- Microscopy: Gram stain revealed gram-positive cocci in chains and gram-positive rods in a branching pattern.  Image Title: Gram Stain of Iris Abscess  Image Description: Mixed flora of gram-positive cocci in pairs and chains and gram-positive bacilli (original magnification ×400)- Cultures: Aerobic culture revealed Streptococcus intermedius. The gram-positive bacilli could not be identified more specifically.- Additional Tests: Blood and urine cultures, electrocardiogram and echocardiogram were unremarkable.\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n\nOptions:\nA. Infectious endophthalmitis (Streptococcus intermedius)\nB. Polymicrobial bacterial iris abscess (Streptococcus intermedius and likely Actinomyces)\nC. Fungal endophthalmitis\nD. Endogenous endophthalmitis with iris abscess\n",
    "root": {
      "label": "Subacute Unilateral Anterior Uveitis with Focal Granuloma and Hypopyon",
      "time_course": "subacute",
      "severity": "unspecified",
